        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Smokeless, Open or Propane</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing</a>
          <a href="#fuel" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Fuel</a>
          <a href="#rules" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Burn Bans and Rules</a>
          <a href="#placement" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Placement and Safety</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Cleaning and Care</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. What It Costs</a>
          <a href="#skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Who Should Skip One</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best fire pit for most patios is a double-wall smokeless wood pit like the Solo Stove Bonfire 2.0, because it gives you a real fire with far less smoke than an open ring, as long as you burn dry, seasoned wood. Go bigger only if you regularly host a crowd, choose a heavier pit if you want to cook over the fire, and choose propane if smoke, ash or burn bans rule out wood. Check your local rules before you buy any of them.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            A fire pit is a simple purchase that people often regret for simple reasons: it smokes everyone out, it is too small for the group, it rusts through in two seasons, or it turns out to be banned on the nights you most want to use it. So instead of ranking every bowl that holds a fire, we looked at the problems that decide whether a fire pit gets used, and chose the strongest option for each kind of owner: the patio entertainer, the big-group host, the outdoor cook, the camper and the person who cannot or will not burn wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pit here is a real, currently sold model, and every figure we quote comes from the manufacturer or a retailer listing, attributed where we use it. We did not lab-test these pits and we do not publish scores we did not measure. Where listings disagreed, as they often did on weight and warranty terms, we say so on the card. Prices in this category swing widely between sellers and seasons, so treat any price as a snapshot.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things. Smoke, because it is the most common complaint about a backyard fire. Size and heat, because a pit that is too small for your group gets crowded and one that is too big wastes wood. Build and warranty, because thin steel burns and rusts through. And fuel and rules, because wood, pellets and propane each suit different homes and different local regulations.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gave us three families. Double-wall smokeless wood pits, led by Solo Stove and Breeo, burn wood with far less smoke than an open ring when the wood is dry. Fan-assisted pits like the BioLite push air into the fire mechanically. Propane fire bowls trade the crackle of wood for no smoke, no ash and, often, permission to burn when wood fires are not allowed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Smokeless, Open Wood or Propane
          </h2>

          <p>
            A so-called smokeless fire pit is really a low-smoke fire pit. The common design uses two steel walls with a gap between them. Air enters through holes at the base, some feeds the fire from below, and the rest heats as it rises between the walls and exits through holes near the top of the fire. That hot air ignites much of the smoke and unburned gases before they leave the pit. The effect is real, but it depends heavily on dry, seasoned wood; wet or green wood will smoke in any pit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A traditional open fire pit or steel ring is cheaper and throws more heat sideways, because the fire is not shielded by double walls. It is also smokier, and the smoke follows people around as the wind changes. For a campfire feel at the lowest cost, an open ring still works, but it is the design most likely to annoy neighbors and to leave you smelling of smoke.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane fire pits burn clean, light instantly and switch off with a valve, with no ash to clean. They produce less radiant heat than a wood fire of the same size and none of the crackle or smell, and you need to budget for propane. Their big practical advantage is that some approved propane appliances may be permitted during burn bans when wood fires are not, though that depends entirely on local rules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Should You Buy?</h3>

          <p>
            If you want a real wood fire on a patio with minimal smoke, buy a double-wall smokeless pit. If you want to cook over the fire, choose a heavier pit with grilling accessories such as the Breeo. If you camp or move the pit often, the BioLite or a small Solo Stove travels best. If you want a fire with no wood, ash or smoke at all, or face frequent burn bans, buy a propane pit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            These six cover the main kinds of fire pit: double-wall smokeless wood pits in two sizes, a heavy cooking-capable pit, a fan-assisted portable pit, a propane bowl and a pellet-friendly quick-lighting pit. We chose them from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and owner reports; we did not lab-test them, and every figure below is attributed to the listing it came from. Weights, warranty terms and prices vary between listings, so check the exact listing before you order.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
