        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing a Fire Pit to Your Group and Space
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fire pit size is usually listed as the outside diameter, and it decides both how many people can sit around it and how much wood it burns. As a rough guide, a pit around 19 to 20 inches across suits two to four people on a small patio, a pit around 24 to 27 inches suits four to eight, and anything larger is for big gatherings with plenty of space and plenty of firewood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bigger is not automatically better. A larger fire burns more wood every hour and throws more heat, which can make the nearest seats uncomfortably hot in mild weather. A pit that is right-sized for your usual group will be used more often than an oversized one that feels like a project to light. If you host large groups only occasionally, a mid-size pit plus a few blankets is often the better compromise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check what log length the pit takes. Smaller smokeless pits need logs cut shorter, which means buying shorter bundles or cutting your own, while larger pits such as the Yukon accept longer logs, around 22 inches by one reviewer's account. If you have a supply of standard firewood, a pit that fits it saves a lot of work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leave plenty of space around the pit. Allow room for chairs set back from the fire, clear walking paths and a generous distance from the house, fences, overhanging branches and anything that can catch fire. Manufacturers give minimum clearances in their manuals, and local codes may set their own; follow whichever is stricter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel: Wood, Pellets, Charcoal or Propane
          </h2>

          <p>
            Firewood quality matters more than the pit. Seasoned hardwood, split and dried for many months so that it is light, cracked at the ends and sounds hollow when knocked together, burns hot and clean. Green or wet wood hisses, smokes heavily and leaves creosote and sooty residue, and no smokeless design can fully fix that. If your pit smokes, the wood is the first thing to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some pits are designed for pellets or pre-made wood packs. Pellets are compressed, very dry wood that burns cleanly and lights easily, which is why owners report especially low smoke with pellets in pits such as the TIKI. They cost more than cordwood per hour of fire, but they remove the need to store, split and dry logs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The BioLite burns both wood and charcoal, and with the included grate it doubles as a small grill. Charcoal gives steady heat for cooking with less flame and smoke than wood. If cooking over the fire is a priority, look at the Breeo with its grilling accessories or at our guide to the <a href="/best-charcoal-grill/">best charcoal grills</a> for a dedicated cooker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane is the most convenient fuel and the least romantic. A standard tank runs a pit for a number of hours depending on the burner's BTU rating and how high you run it. You pay for propane each time, but there is nothing to store, split, light or clean, and the fire goes out the instant you close the valve.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Burn Bans, Local Rules and Neighbors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before buying any wood-burning fire pit, check your local rules. Many towns and counties restrict recreational fires, set minimum distances from buildings and property lines, limit the size of the fire, or ban open burning entirely during dry or high-wind periods. Homeowner associations and rental agreements often add their own restrictions. A quick call to your local fire department or a look at your municipality's website saves a costly mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Burn bans are where fuel type matters most. During high fire danger, wood fires are commonly prohibited outright. Some jurisdictions allow approved gas appliances with an on/off valve during certain restrictions, and listings for the Outland Firebowl note that its CSA approval is why it can be used during many campfire bans. That is never automatic, though: some bans prohibit all open flame, so always check the specific restriction in force.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smoke affects neighbors as much as it affects your guests. Even a low-smoke pit produces some smoke while it lights and if the wood is damp. Being considerate means burning dry wood, avoiding fires on still, humid evenings when smoke hangs low, and not burning trash, treated lumber or yard waste, which are often prohibited and produce harmful smoke.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you live in an area prone to wildfires, the safest choice may be a propane pit or no fire at all during the dry season. Follow local guidance and err on the side of caution.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Placement, Surfaces and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Place a fire pit on a level, stable, non-combustible surface, well away from structures, trees and anything flammable. The National Fire Protection Association and fire pit manufacturers generally advise keeping fire features well away from homes and overhangs, and many manuals specify a minimum clearance; follow the larger of your manual's figure and your local code. Never use a fire pit under a covered porch, awning or low branches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not set a wood fire pit directly on a wooden deck unless the manufacturer explicitly approves it with a specific heat shield or stand. Even on concrete and pavers, heat and embers can discolor or damage the surface, and Corten steel pits can leave rust stains. A stand, a heat shield or a fire-resistant pad protects the surface; our guides to the <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">best paver sealers</a> and <a href="/best-pool-deck-sealer/">pool deck sealers</a> help protect the slab around it, and a <a href="/best-pressure-washer-for-concrete/">pressure washer for concrete</a> cleans up soot and ash marks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep children and pets supervised and away from the pit, and never leave a fire unattended. Keep a hose, bucket of water or fire extinguisher within reach. Do not use petrol or lighter fluid to start or revive a wood fire; use kindling and fire starters instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put the fire out completely before you leave it. For wood fires, let the fire burn down, then douse it with water or smother it and stir the embers until everything is cold to the touch, following your manufacturer's guidance, since some stainless pits warn against sudden cooling. Store cold ash in a lidded metal container away from the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cleaning and Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Empty the ash regularly once it is completely cold. Ash holds moisture and is corrosive against steel, and a pit full of ash also burns less efficiently because it chokes the airflow that smokeless designs depend on. Pits with a removable ash pan, such as the Solo Stove 2.0 models and the TIKI, make this quick; pits without one need scooping or a vacuum designed for cold ash.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stainless steel pits will discolor with heat, turning golden, blue or brown. That is a normal effect of heating stainless steel and does not mean the pit is failing. Corten steel is designed to form a protective rust-colored patina, which can run off onto the surface underneath when it rains, so use a stand or pad if you care about the patio.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cover the pit when it is cold and not in use, or store it in a garage or shed. Rain filling the pit and sitting in the ash is the main cause of corrosion. For propane pits, check the hose and connections for leaks at the start of each season with soapy water, and store the tank outdoors, never in a garage or basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check for damage once or twice a season: warped or cracked walls, burned-through spots, loose legs and failing welds. Any of those mean the pit is no longer safe and should be repaired or replaced.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. What a Fire Pit Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fire pit prices span a wide range. Basic steel rings cost very little; double-wall smokeless pits run from a couple of hundred dollars for small models to several hundred for large ones; heavy Corten designs like the Breeo, which Ace Hardware lists at $599.99, sit at the top. Propane pits vary from compact portable bowls to large built-in tables.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running costs depend on fuel. Firewood is cheapest if you buy it by the cord or have your own supply, and expensive if you buy small kiln-dried bundles at a store. Pellets and pre-made wood packs cost more per hour but save storage and effort. Propane costs depend on local prices and how high you run the burner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add the accessories you will actually use: a cover, a stand or heat shield if the pit sits on pavers or composite, a poker, and for cooking pits a grill grate. Spark screens, which reduce embers escaping, are worth considering in windy or dry areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider longevity. Thin painted steel pits can rust or burn through in a few seasons. Stainless and Corten pits from established makers typically come with longer warranties, which is part of what you pay for. A pit that lasts ten years is usually cheaper in the end than three that each last three.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Skip a Fire Pit
          </h2>

          <p>
            A fire pit is not right for every home. If your local rules prohibit recreational fires, if you live in a wildfire-prone area with frequent bans, or if your outdoor space is a small balcony or a deck close to the house, a wood fire pit is the wrong choice. A propane pit, where permitted, or an outdoor heater may suit you better.
          </p>

          <p>
            If smoke is a particular concern for someone in your household, for example because of asthma or other respiratory conditions, a propane pit avoids wood smoke entirely. If you mainly want to cook outdoors, a grill or smoker does that job better; see our guides to the <a href="/best-charcoal-grill/">best charcoal grills</a>, the <a href="/best-smoker/">best smokers</a> and the <a href="/best-electric-smoker/">best electric smokers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, a good fire pit becomes the center of the backyard. A right-sized smokeless pit with dry wood, set on a protected surface at a safe distance from the house, gives you a fire you will use most evenings of the season without smoking out your guests or your neighbors.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are planning a dedicated fire pit area, think about the surface first. A level, non-combustible area of concrete or pavers is ideal; our <a href="/concrete-patio-cost/">concrete patio cost</a> guide and the comparison of <a href="/pavers-vs-concrete/">pavers versus concrete</a> help you plan and budget the space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Burning wet or green wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No smokeless design can burn wet wood cleanly. Use seasoned hardwood that is light, cracked at the ends and dry, and store it off the ground under cover so it stays that way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring local burn rules</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many areas restrict recreational fires or ban them during dry periods, and HOAs often add their own rules. Check before you buy, and check again on the night you plan to burn.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting a wood pit on a deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most wood-burning pits are not approved for wooden or composite decks. Embers and radiant heat can scorch or ignite the surface; use the manufacturer's approved stand and heat shield, or keep the pit on stone or concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying too big a pit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A larger pit burns more wood and can make close seats too hot. Size the pit for your usual group, not the biggest party of the year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving ash in the pit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ash holds moisture, corrodes steel and chokes the airflow that smokeless pits rely on. Empty cold ash into a lidded metal container regularly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

