          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do smokeless fire pits really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They significantly reduce smoke once the fire is hot and burning dry wood, by burning off smoke with heated secondary air near the top of the pit. They still smoke while lighting and with damp wood, so wood quality matters as much as the design.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size fire pit do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pit around 19 to 20 inches suits two to four people, around 24 to 27 inches suits four to eight, and larger pits suit big gatherings. A larger pit burns more wood, so size it for your usual group rather than occasional parties.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a fire pit during a burn ban?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wood fires are usually prohibited during burn bans. Some jurisdictions allow approved gas appliances with a shut-off valve during certain restrictions, but others ban all open flame. Always check the specific restriction with your local fire department.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far should a fire pit be from my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the larger of your manufacturer's stated clearance and your local fire code. Keep the pit well away from walls, fences, overhangs and trees, and never use it under a covered porch or awning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a fire pit on a wooden deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no, unless the manufacturer specifically approves it with a particular stand or heat shield. Embers and radiant heat can damage or ignite wood and composite decking, so a stone, concrete or paver surface is much safer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a propane fire pit as warm as a wood fire?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Propane pits produce less radiant heat than a wood fire of similar size and are more about ambience. Their advantages are no smoke, no ash, instant on and off, and possible use during some burn restrictions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I put out a fire pit safely?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Let the fire burn down, then extinguish it with water or by smothering, and stir until the embers are cold to the touch, following your manufacturer's guidance. Store cold ash in a lidded metal container away from the house.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

